id: dirty-trick
name: Dirty Trick
type: standard
actionCost: 1
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: true
special: true
usageLimit: 1/round
clashSkill: bluff
clashDefenderSkills: intuition
dirtyTrick: true
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Cheat. A Clash of your Bluff against their Intuition, and winning buys one of three:
  Blinded, Guard Down until you are hit, or Compelled against somebody of your choosing.
text: >
  Dirty Trick Maneuver [1/Round]: Refusing to accept failure, you’ll cheat, lie, and
  connive your way to victory, whether by throwing dirt in your opponent’s eyes,
  declaring the presence of a powerful figure, or emotionally manipulating an ally by
  killing their family and making it look like the enemy did it.
  –Maneuver Type: Standard Maneuver
  –Action Cost: 1 Action
  –Minions: N/A
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Exploitable: N/A
  –Effect: When you use this Special Maneuver, target a Character within a Sphere AoE
  (centered on you). Make a Clash (Bluff vs Intuition) against that Character. If you
  win, apply one of the following effects:

  * Pocket Sand: Your target gains the Blinded Combat Condition until the end of your
  turn.

  * Made ya look!: Your target gains the Guard Down Combat Condition until the end of
  your turn or until they are hit by an Attacking Maneuver (whichever comes first).

  * It’s Such a Tragedy!: Your target gains the Compelled Combat Condition until the end
  of their turn against a target of your choice. Additionally, if you scored a Critical
  Result or your target scored a Botch Result in your Clash, they must either use the
  Transformation Maneuver or Power Up Maneuver during their next turn. You can only use
  this effect once per Combat Encounter.

---

# --- The Clash -------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "A Clash (Bluff vs Intuition)" - the first Skill Clash in these rules with a different
# Skill on each side. Every other one names one Skill and both sides roll it, which is why
# a Clash only ever carried one. `clashDefenderSkills` is what the other side may answer
# with, and a Clash with none behaves exactly as it always did.
#
# A list of one here. It is a list because the Feint Maneuver names two with a slash
# between them - "(Bluff vs Intuition/Perception)" - and that slash is a choice, the same
# one as in "Strike/Dodge". One in the list is a defender with nothing to decide.
#
# A tie goes to the Defender, here as everywhere else: cheating has to be done outright.
#
# --- Where it reaches ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Target a Character within a Sphere AoE (centered on you)." Said and not policed, like
# every other range in this system: nothing here moves a token and nothing here measures
# one. The Maneuver takes a target and the table agrees whether they are inside it.
#
# Worth saying that it is a Character rather than an Opponent. An ally is a legal target,
# which the third effect leans on - "emotionally manipulating an ALLY".
#
# --- The three effects ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Offered on the card once the Clash is won, the way the Thrust Maneuver's two are. Not
# chosen when the Maneuver is declared: "if you win, apply one of the following effects" is
# a choice made after the dice.
#
# Pocket Sand and Made ya look! are both "until the end of YOUR turn", so the clock is kept
# by whoever played the trick and the Condition sits on whoever it was played on. It's Such
# a Tragedy! is "until the end of THEIR turn", so that clock is the target's own. Three
# lines, two different characters counting, and the entry says which each time.
#
# --- "Or until they are hit by an Attacking Maneuver" -----------------------------------------
#
# The first duration in these rules with two ways to end. The turn edge is counted as it
# always was, and `until` names the Moment that can end it first - `being-hit`, which is
# already fired on a target the moment a Strike beats their defence.
#
# Swept by the GM's client alone, as the turn edges are. With no GM connected the Guard
# Down still comes off at the end of your turn, which is the other half of "whichever comes
# first" - it lasts longer than it should rather than sticking for ever.
#
# --- The Compelled target -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Against a target of your choice." Read off whoever you have targeted when you choose the
# effect, and written on the card. Not tracked, because Compelled's own Slot has said from
# the day it was written that who you were told to attack is the table's to keep: "said to
# the table, not enforced". This gives the table the sentence to keep.
#
# --- The rider, and what is not built ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# "They must either use the Transformation Maneuver or Power Up Maneuver during their next
# turn." Said, not enforced, and it could not be enforced anyway: there is no Transformation
# Maneuver in this system, so half of the choice does not exist to be offered. The condition
# for it is real and is checked - a Critical of yours or a Botch of theirs, read off the
# Clash that was just rolled - and the card says so when it happens.
#
# "Once per Combat Encounter", and only this effect. The Maneuver is 1/Round and this is a
# third limit inside it, so it is counted against its own name rather than the Maneuver's:
# a per-Encounter tally that a new Round leaves alone and a new Encounter clears, which is
# what the round reset already promises anything not written `round:`.
#
# --- How it is gained -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A Special Maneuver, so nobody has it until something hands it over. No Skill opens it
# yet. Bluff is the obvious candidate - it is the Skill the Clash is made with - but the
# Bluff entry is what says so, and it has not been read here. Attack Absorption is waiting
# on the same kind of line.
